---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import landscape from '../assets/images/night-mountain.jpg';

const team = (await getCollection('team')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const teamMembers = await Promise.all(
	team.map(async (member) => {
		const rendered = await render(member);
		return { member, Content: rendered.Content };
	}),
);
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About"
	description="The Solar Wind Sherpas are scientists and explorers who have spent three decades travelling to total solar eclipses to study the corona and the solar wind."
>
	<article class="page">
		<div class="page-narrow">
			<p class="label page-kicker">About</p>
			<h1>Scientists at the edge of the shadow</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				An international team founded in 1995 by Dr. Shadia R. Habbal. We carry observatories to
				the path of totality — often to places with no observatory at all.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="page-hero-image reveal">
			<Image
				src={landscape}
				alt="Snow-capped mountain peaks under a dark, star-filled sky, with mist along the valley floor"
				widths={[900, 1400, 2000]}
				sizes="(max-width: 1120px) 100vw, 1120px"
				loading="eager"
			/>
		</div>

		<div class="page-narrow prose">
			<p>
				The Solar Wind Sherpas began as a small group of solar physicists who understood that the
				corona — the Sun’s outer atmosphere — is only fully visible to the naked eye, and to certain
				optical diagnostics, during a total solar eclipse. Space-borne coronagraphs occult the inner
				corona. Totality does not.
			</p>
			<p>
				The name is literal. Each expedition moves specialised imaging systems, spectrographs,
				tracking mounts, and power into remote desert, ice, mountain, and coastal sites chosen for
				the geometry of the umbra. The team has grown from six people to more than thirty scientists,
				engineers, and observers from around the world.
			</p>
			<p>
				What unites the group is a single physical problem: why the corona is hotter than a million
				degrees, how the solar wind is launched, and how magnetic structure in that region shapes
				space weather at Earth. The work is published. The method is still to stand in the shadow
				with instruments that have been aligned in the hours before second contact.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="page-narrow">
			{
				teamMembers.map(({ member, Content }) => (
					<section class="team-card reveal">
						<p class="role">{member.data.role}</p>
						<h3>{member.data.name}</h3>
						{member.data.affiliation && <p>{member.data.affiliation}</p>}
						<Content />
					</section>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>
